            "textId": "done-block:paper-mock-jiangsu-nantong-2026-01-senior-three-academic-quality-monitoring:grammar-fill:1",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-nantong-2026-01-senior-three-academic-quality-monitoring:grammar-fill:1",
            "text": "Maijishan Grottoes is one of the four greatest grottoes in China. Different from the other three, Maijishan Grottoes are a series of caves, excavated (开凿) into the side of the mountain Maijishan. The caves are undoubtedly a large sculpture museum."
          },
          {
            "textId": "done-block:paper-mock-jiangsu-nantong-2026-01-senior-three-academic-quality-monitoring:grammar-fill:2",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-nantong-2026-01-senior-three-academic-quality-monitoring:grammar-fill:2",
            "text": "Inside the 194 caves are 7,200 statues, whose heights vary from 20 centimeters to 15 meters. The rock here is too soft, so the figures are, instead, clay models. The few sculptures created from rock have been brought in from elsewhere. Judging from the figures and costumes of the statues, most of them reflect the characteristics of Han nationality."
          },
          {
            "textId": "done-block:paper-mock-jiangsu-nantong-2026-01-senior-three-academic-quality-monitoring:grammar-fill:3",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-nantong-2026-01-senior-three-academic-quality-monitoring:grammar-fill:3",
            "text": "The grottoes were carved over a period of 1.500 years, starting in 384 AD, right up to the beginning of the twentieth century. The caves were not easily accessible, thus escaping much of the destruction throughout tumultuous (动荡的) periods in history. The stairs leading up and around the grottoes were originally made of wood but these have been steadily replaced by metal supports for safety reasons."
          },
          {
            "textId": "done-block:paper-mock-jiangsu-nantong-2026-01-senior-three-academic-quality-monitoring:grammar-fill:4",
            "paragraphId": "block:paper-mock-jiangsu-nantong-2026-01-senior-three-academic-quality-monitoring:grammar-fill:4",
            "text": "The sculptures represent the phased development of Chinese sculpture and architecture. The earliest examples demonstrate an influence of Indian design, with later native Chinese styles becoming more popular. All designs are dedicated to the pursuit of Buddhism. The sculptures have helped archeologists to not only track the growth and advance of Chinese sculpture but the history and expansion of Buddhism in China."
          }
        ],
